From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 00:30:03 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vulkan: sleep between the polls of spin_wait_for_sync_file()

A driver without timeline syncobjs (v3dv) waits for a binary syncobj to
get a fence in spin_wait_for_sync_file(): a sync_file export and a
zero-timeout wait, then sched_yield(), until the signal operation has
been submitted. The loop never sleeps, so while that submission is held
up it keeps its CPU and runs again whenever the CPU is free.

On a Raspberry Pi 5 Xen DomD (2026-10-24) a Venus ring thread of the AGL
cluster's context held a whole CPU for 20 minutes the way this loop
does: 80% of it in the kernel, and 1.66 million context switches, all
but 12,000 of them involuntary (a yield leaves the task runnable). Its
context's other ring thread was blocked on the V3D mutex, held by weston
inside a V3D submit on the same CPU; weston stayed queued there with the
lowest vruntime and did not run again, and the device model of the
Android guest, whose main loop waits for the same mutex, stopped the
guest the whole time. The ring thread's stack was not sampled. That ring
waiting for the other is what this loop is for (see 0003); nothing else
in the Vulkan runtime or v3dv yields in a loop, and the render server's
ring yields no more with VKR_RING_RELAX="0,100".

Yield for the first 8 polls, as before, then sleep, from 10 us doubling
up to 1 ms and no longer than the time left (rounded up to a whole
microsecond, plus the timer slack). A wait that ends within the first
polls costs what it did; a longer one gives the CPU up between polls, at
most doubling the time the signal took to arrive, and up to 1 ms more.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/src/vulkan/runtime/vk_drm_syncobj.c
+++ b/src/vulkan/runtime/vk_drm_syncobj.c
@@ -237,6 +237,31 @@
    }
 }
 
+/* Between two polls of spin_wait_for_sync_file(): yield the first 8 times,
+ * then sleep, from 10 us doubling up to 1 ms and no longer than the time left.
+ *
+ * A yield-only loop never sleeps, so while the signal operation it waits for
+ * is held up it keeps its CPU and runs again as soon as the CPU is free. When
+ * whatever holds that up needs the same CPU, it stalls both: a Venus ring
+ * thread waiting here for its context's other ring, which was itself blocked
+ * on the V3D mutex, kept the CPU from the compositor that held the mutex, on a
+ * Raspberry Pi 5 DomD for 20 minutes.
+ */
+static void
+spin_wait_relax(uint32_t *iter, uint64_t abs_timeout_ns)
+{
+   if (++*iter <= 8) {
+      sched_yield();
+      return;
+   }
+
+   uint64_t sleep_ns = MIN2(10000ull << MIN2(*iter - 9, 7), 1000000ull);
+   const uint64_t now = os_time_get_nano();
+   if (now < abs_timeout_ns)
+      sleep_ns = MIN2(sleep_ns, abs_timeout_ns - now);
+   os_time_sleep(DIV_ROUND_UP(sleep_ns, 1000));
+}
+
 static VkResult
 spin_wait_for_sync_file(struct vk_device *device,
                         uint32_t wait_count,
@@ -245,6 +270,8 @@
                         uint64_t abs_timeout_ns)
 {
    if (wait_flags & VK_SYNC_WAIT_ANY) {
+      uint32_t iter = 0;
+
       while (1) {
          for (uint32_t i = 0; i < wait_count; i++) {
             VkResult result = sync_has_sync_file(device, waits[i].sync);
@@ -255,10 +282,12 @@
          if (os_time_get_nano() >= abs_timeout_ns)
             return VK_TIMEOUT;
 
-         sched_yield();
+         spin_wait_relax(&iter, abs_timeout_ns);
       }
    } else {
       for (uint32_t i = 0; i < wait_count; i++) {
+         uint32_t iter = 0;
+
          while (1) {
             VkResult result = sync_has_sync_file(device, waits[i].sync);
             if (result == VK_SUCCESS)
@@ -270,7 +299,7 @@
             if (os_time_get_nano() >= abs_timeout_ns)
                return VK_TIMEOUT;
 
-            sched_yield();
+            spin_wait_relax(&iter, abs_timeout_ns);
          }
       }
    }
